\chapter{Rope model}

The rope is a chain of eighty links, each a small ball, joined neighbour to
neighbour. Each joint keeps neighbouring links within one link spacing of each
other. Every pair of links collides, neighbours included. A joint alone only
stops links from moving apart, so without neighbour contact a link could pass
through the one beside it.

The links are drawn as one continuous tube through their centres, so the
spacing has to be small against the radius. With forty links at 2.5 radii
apart, the straight piece between two neighbours came within half a diameter
of a piece further along the rope while every pair of centres stayed clear. The
rope visibly passed through itself when a knot was pulled tight. Eighty links
at 1.25 radii keep the tube from crossing itself under the same pulls, for the
same length of rope and about twice the physics cost (1.5 ms a frame).

Both ends are free. Nothing holds the rope in place, so the player moves it by
grabbing any link. Gravity is a switch: off by default, so the rope floats and
can be tied in the air, and on, so the rope falls onto the floor. The rope model
knows nothing of three.js or React.

The numbers come from the decision gate in the architecture chapter. Solver
iterations, damping and the speed cap are the values that passed it. The cap is
applied after every physics step. It is a deliberate departure from pure
physics, and the gate result records why.

\section{Shapes the rest of the code reads}

The rope model returns a snapshot: the link centres in order, from the first
link to the last, and the link radius. Anchors and the floor are boxes in the
scene. The model takes them as plain shapes, so it does not depend on what they
are for.

@d Rope: snapshot types @{
export interface Vec3 {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  z: number;
}

// The link centres in order, from the first link to the last.
export interface RopeSnapshot {
  readonly points: readonly Vec3[];
  readonly radius: number;
}

// An axis-aligned box given by its centre and its half sizes.
export interface Box {
  center: Vec3;
  half: Vec3;
}
@}

\section{Physics model}

The physics engine is loaded once and shared. A rope is created in a world of
its own, so disposing of a rope frees exactly what it made.

Each link is a dynamic ball with continuous collision detection on, so a fast
drag cannot pass a link through another. The physics runs in four substeps per frame. Measured on a trefoil falling onto
the floor, one substep let two links that are not neighbours come within 0.029 m
of each other, where contact is 0.040 m. That is a pass-through, and it let the
knot slip apart. Four substeps keep the closest pair at 0.039 m. Linear damping
slows each link, so a
released rope settles instead of wobbling. Measured after a release, a damping of
0.5 took about six seconds to settle, 2 took under two, and 4 took under one. The
value is 4. The speed cap is applied to every link
after the step, and the step returns the fastest link speed, which the page uses
to decide when the rope has settled.

A grab steers the held link toward the hand by setting its velocity before each
step. It is not a joint. A joint pulls its link with a force the contacts cannot
overcome, so a held link would pass through a bar it was pushed against. A
velocity does give way to a contact, so the link stops at the bar. The grab
starts at the link's current position, so picking a link never moves the rope.
Releasing stops the steering. The rest of the rope follows the held link through
the joints, as a real rope follows a hand.

The held point moves toward the hand at no more than 1.5 m/s, and the link is
steered toward it. Steering the link straight to the hand is what made the rope
turbulent. On a 0.3 m flick of the mouse, the peak link speed was 1.3 m/s when
steered straight to the hand, and 0.7 m/s with the limit. The limit is fast
enough to follow a quick hand, so the held point stays under the cursor.

@d Rope: physics model @{
import RAPIER from '@@dimforge/rapier3d-compat';
import type { Box, RopeSnapshot, Vec3 } from './snapshot';

export const LINK_COUNT = 80;
export const LINK_RADIUS = 0.02;
export const LINK_SPACING = 0.025;
export const MAX_LINK_SPEED = 3;
const SOLVER_ITERATIONS = 32;
// Physics steps per frame. Links move about a diameter per step when the rope falls,
// and with one step they pass into each other. Four steps stop that.
const SUBSTEPS = 4;
const LINEAR_DAMPING = 4;
// Each link has half the density it had at forty links, so the whole rope weighs
// the same as before. Eighty links at full density made the rope feel twice as heavy.
const LINK_DENSITY = 0.5;
// The held point follows the hand no faster than this, so a quick flick cannot throw the rope.
const HAND_SPEED = 1.5;
// The held link is steered toward the held point at this rate, per second.
const STEER_GAIN = 8;
const GRAVITY = -9.81;

export interface Rope {
  grabLink(index: number, target: Vec3): void;
  moveGrab(target: Vec3): void;
  releaseGrab(): void;
  setGravity(on: boolean): void;
  step(): number;
  snapshot(): RopeSnapshot;
  dispose(): void;
}

let ready: Promise<void> | null = null;

export function initPhysics(): Promise<void> {
  ready ??= RAPIER.init();
  return ready;
}

// A rope lying straight from start along the x axis, with its first link at start.
// With a pose, the links start at the given centres instead; the tests use this to start from a knot.
export function createRope(start: Vec3, obstacles: readonly Box[], pose?: Vec3[]): Rope {
  const world = new RAPIER.World({ x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 });
  world.numSolverIterations = SOLVER_ITERATIONS;
  world.timestep = 1 / 60 / SUBSTEPS;

  const links: RAPIER.RigidBody[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < LINK_COUNT; i++) {
    const at = pose?.[i] ?? { x: start.x + i * LINK_SPACING, y: start.y, z: start.z };
    const body = world.createRigidBody(
      RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()
        .setTranslation(at.x, at.y, at.z)
        .setLinearDamping(LINEAR_DAMPING)
        .setCcdEnabled(true),
    );
    world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.ball(LINK_RADIUS).setDensity(LINK_DENSITY), body);
    links.push(body);
  }

  const origin = { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 };
  for (let i = 0; i < LINK_COUNT - 1; i++) {
    world.createImpulseJoint(RAPIER.JointData.rope(LINK_SPACING, origin, origin), links[i], links[i + 1], true);
  }

  for (const box of obstacles) {
    const body = world.createRigidBody(
      RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.fixed().setTranslation(box.center.x, box.center.y, box.center.z),
    );
    world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(box.half.x, box.half.y, box.half.z), body);
  }

  // The held link, where the hand has asked it to be (goal), and the point it is
  // steered toward (point), which follows the goal no faster than HAND_SPEED.
  let held: { index: number; goal: Vec3; point: Vec3 } | null = null;

  const releaseGrab = () => {
    held = null;
  };

  // Steers the held link toward its goal by setting its velocity. A contact can
  // still take that velocity away, so the link stops at a bar instead of passing it.
  const steerHeld = () => {
    if (!held) return;
    const link = links[held.index];
    const at = link.translation();
    const dt = world.timestep;
    const { goal, point } = held;
    const dx = goal.x - point.x;
    const dy = goal.y - point.y;
    const dz = goal.z - point.z;
    const gap = Math.hypot(dx, dy, dz);
    if (gap > 0) {
      const move = Math.min(gap, HAND_SPEED * dt) / gap;
      point.x += dx * move;
      point.y += dy * move;
      point.z += dz * move;
    }
    const v = {
      x: STEER_GAIN * (point.x - at.x),
      y: STEER_GAIN * (point.y - at.y),
      z: STEER_GAIN * (point.z - at.z),
    };
    const speed = Math.hypot(v.x, v.y, v.z);
    if (speed > MAX_LINK_SPEED) {
      const scale = MAX_LINK_SPEED / speed;
      v.x *= scale;
      v.y *= scale;
      v.z *= scale;
    }
    link.setLinvel(v, true);
  };

  return {
    grabLink(index, target) {
      held = { index, goal: { ...target }, point: { ...links[index].translation() } };
    },
    moveGrab(target) {
      if (held) held.goal = { ...target };
    },
    releaseGrab,
    setGravity(on) {
      world.gravity = on ? { x: 0, y: GRAVITY, z: 0 } : { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 };
      // A settled link sleeps, and a sleeping link ignores a change of gravity until it wakes.
      for (const body of links) body.wakeUp();
    },
    step() {
      let fastest = 0;
      for (let k = 0; k < SUBSTEPS; k++) {
        steerHeld();
        world.step();
        for (const body of links) {
          const v = body.linvel();
          const speed = Math.hypot(v.x, v.y, v.z);
          if (speed > MAX_LINK_SPEED) {
            const scale = MAX_LINK_SPEED / speed;
            body.setLinvel({ x: v.x * scale, y: v.y * scale, z: v.z * scale }, true);
          }
          fastest = Math.max(fastest, Math.min(speed, MAX_LINK_SPEED));
        }
      }
      return fastest;
    },
    snapshot() {
      return { points: links.map((body) => body.translation()), radius: LINK_RADIUS };
    },
    dispose() {
      world.free();
    },
  };
}
@}

\section{Tests}

The tests carry the gate criteria forward, so a later change cannot quietly
break the rope. A link may stretch by at most five per cent of the spacing. A
drag must keep every link under the speed cap. A held link must not pass through
a bar. A released rope must come to rest, which is the check against wobble.

@d Rope: tests @{
import { beforeAll, describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { LINK_COUNT, LINK_RADIUS, LINK_SPACING, MAX_LINK_SPEED, createRope, initPhysics } from './rope';
import type { Vec3 } from './snapshot';

const START: Vec3 = { x: -0.975, y: 0.2, z: 0 };
const FLOOR = { center: { x: 0, y: -1, z: 0 }, half: { x: 2, y: 0.05, z: 2 } };

// The standard trefoil curve as a pose for the rope, its links spaced evenly along it.
function trefoilPose(): Vec3[] {
  const f = (t: number): Vec3 => ({
    x: Math.sin(t) + 2 * Math.sin(2 * t),
    y: Math.cos(t) - 2 * Math.cos(2 * t),
    z: -Math.sin(3 * t),
  });
  const N = 20000;
  const T = 2 * Math.PI * 0.97;
  const pts = Array.from({ length: N + 1 }, (_, i) => f((T * i) / N));
  const cum = [0];
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
    const a = pts[i - 1];
    const b = pts[i];
    cum.push(cum[i - 1] + Math.hypot(b.x - a.x, b.y - a.y, b.z - a.z));
  }
  const scale = (LINK_SPACING * (LINK_COUNT - 1)) / cum[N];
  const out: Vec3[] = [];
  for (let k = 0; k < LINK_COUNT; k++) {
    const target = k * LINK_SPACING;
    let j = 1;
    while (j < N && cum[j] * scale < target) j++;
    const p = pts[j];
    out.push({ x: p.x * scale, y: p.y * scale, z: p.z * scale });
  }
  return out;
}

// The largest neighbour distance, divided by the spacing. One means taut.
function longestStretch(points: readonly Vec3[]): number {
  let longest = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    const a = points[i];
    const b = points[i + 1];
    longest = Math.max(longest, Math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z) / LINK_SPACING);
  }
  return longest;
}

beforeAll(async () => {
  await initPhysics();
});

describe('rope model', () => {
  it('lies still with gravity off and no link stretched beyond five per cent', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const { points } = rope.snapshot();
    expect(points).toHaveLength(LINK_COUNT);
    expect(longestStretch(points)).toBeLessThan(1.05);
    expect(rope.step()).toBeLessThan(0.01);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('falls onto the floor with gravity on', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, [FLOOR]);
    rope.setGravity(true);
    for (let s = 0; s < 600; s++) rope.step();
    const lowest = Math.min(...rope.snapshot().points.map((p) => p.y));
    expect(lowest).toBeGreaterThan(FLOOR.center.y + FLOOR.half.y - 0.01);
    expect(lowest).toBeLessThan(FLOOR.center.y + FLOOR.half.y + LINK_RADIUS + 0.01);
    expect(longestStretch(rope.snapshot().points)).toBeLessThan(1.05);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('falls when gravity is switched on after it has settled', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, [FLOOR]);
    for (let s = 0; s < 600; s++) rope.step();
    const settled = Math.min(...rope.snapshot().points.map((p) => p.y));
    rope.setGravity(true);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    expect(Math.min(...rope.snapshot().points.map((p) => p.y))).toBeLessThan(settled - 0.1);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('does not let links pass through each other when a knot falls', () => {
    const rope = createRope({ x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }, [FLOOR], trefoilPose());
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    rope.setGravity(true);
    let closest = Infinity;
    for (let s = 0; s < 600; s++) {
      rope.step();
      const p = rope.snapshot().points;
      for (let i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {
        for (let j = i + 2; j < p.length; j++) {
          closest = Math.min(closest, Math.hypot(p[i].x - p[j].x, p[i].y - p[j].y, p[i].z - p[j].z));
        }
      }
    }
    // Contact is two radii apart. Allowing five per cent of overlap is generous.
    expect(closest).toBeGreaterThan(2 * LINK_RADIUS * 0.95);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('picks up a link without moving it', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const before = rope.snapshot().points[20];
    rope.grabLink(20, before);
    rope.step();
    const after = rope.snapshot().points[20];
    expect(Math.hypot(after.x - before.x, after.y - before.y, after.z - before.z)).toBeLessThan(0.005);
    rope.releaseGrab();
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('keeps every link under the speed cap while a middle link is dragged in a circle', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const start = rope.snapshot().points[20];
    rope.grabLink(20, start);
    const radius = 0.4;
    const omega = 1.5 / radius;
    let fastest = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < 360; s++) {
      const t = s / 60;
      rope.moveGrab({
        x: start.x + radius * Math.sin(omega * t),
        y: start.y,
        z: start.z + radius * (1 - Math.cos(omega * t)),
      });
      fastest = Math.max(fastest, rope.step());
    }
    expect(fastest).toBeLessThanOrEqual(MAX_LINK_SPEED);
    expect(longestStretch(rope.snapshot().points)).toBeLessThan(1.05);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('comes to rest after it is released', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const start = rope.snapshot().points[20];
    rope.grabLink(20, start);
    for (let s = 0; s < 120; s++) {
      rope.moveGrab({ x: start.x, y: start.y + 0.3 * Math.sin(s / 20), z: start.z });
      rope.step();
    }
    rope.releaseGrab();
    let fastest = 1;
    for (let s = 0; s < 600; s++) fastest = rope.step();
    expect(fastest).toBeLessThan(0.02);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('does not let a quick flick of the mouse throw the rope', () => {
    const rope = createRope(START, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const p0 = rope.snapshot().points[20];
    rope.grabLink(20, p0);
    let peak = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < 240; s++) {
      // The hand jumps 0.3 m in one frame, then stays still.
      rope.moveGrab({ x: p0.x + (s >= 10 ? 0.3 : 0), y: p0.y, z: 0 });
      peak = Math.max(peak, rope.step());
    }
    expect(peak).toBeLessThan(1);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('does not pass a held link through a bar', () => {
    // A bar lies across the rope's path at its height, and the held link is pushed up through it.
    const held = 25;
    const x = START.x + held * LINK_SPACING;
    const bar = { center: { x, y: 0.4, z: 0 }, half: { x: 0.02, y: 0.02, z: 0.4 } };
    const rope = createRope(START, [bar]);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const start = rope.snapshot().points[held];
    rope.grabLink(held, start);
    let highest = -Infinity;
    for (let s = 0; s < 180; s++) {
      rope.moveGrab({ x: start.x, y: 0.8, z: 0 });
      rope.step();
      highest = Math.max(highest, rope.snapshot().points[held].y);
    }
    // The bar's underside is at y = 0.38, so a link under it stays below 0.38 - radius of contact.
    expect(highest).toBeLessThan(bar.center.y - bar.half.y);
    rope.dispose();
  });
});
@}
